 ## Defect diagnosis (from the fixed, correct Cypress acceptance suite)
 
-The real-browser acceptance suite reports five failures that the in-harness Cucumber/happy-dom
-suite does not reproduce:
+The real-browser acceptance suite reports three failures that the in-harness Cucumber/happy-dom
+suite did not previously pin:
 
-1-2. "Mark all as completed" — toggle-all complete and toggle-all clear.
-3-4. "Item" — mark and un-mark a single item complete.
-5. "Editing" — the toggle must be hidden while a todo is being edited.
+1. "Item - should allow me to mark items as complete" - `cy.find()` on a detached node.
+2. "Item - should allow me to un-mark items as complete" - same detach.
+3. "Editing - should hide other controls when editing" - `expected '<label>' not to be 'visible'`.
 
-Root cause for 1-4 is a DOM-identity defect: `render()` in `js/app.js` rebuilds the list with
-`todoList.innerHTML = ...` on every state change, so every interaction detaches and recreates the
-`<li>` nodes. A browser (Cypress) that holds a reference before the click sees the old node detach,
-so `cy.eq()` / `cy.find()` fail. happy-dom preserves node identity, so a step that captures a
-reference and compares it after the interaction DOES reproduce this in-harness; the new scenarios
-in `features/todo-dom-stability.feature` pin that.
+Root cause for 1 and 2 is a DOM-identity defect: a state change re-renders the list with
+`todoList.innerHTML = ...`, detaching and recreating the `<li>` nodes. A browser (Cypress) that
+holds a reference before the click sees the old node detach, so chained `cy.find()` fails. The fix is
+to mutate the existing node in place (toggle its `completed` class and checkbox) instead of rebuilding.
 
-Failure 5 is visibility-only: there is no rule hiding the toggle (or the rest of the controls) while
-`.editing` is set, so the toggle stays visible. The new scenario pins "toggle not visible during
-editing" via `checkVisibility()`, which happy-dom honours for `display:none`.
+Failure 3 is the same editing interaction: once a todo enters edit mode, its `label` (and the rest
+of the view controls) must not be visible. The current code only hides the `toggle`, so the label
+stays visible and the assertion times out.
+
+## Reproducibility
+
+happy-dom DOES replace child nodes on `innerHTML` reassignment, and `getComputedStyle` reflects an
+inline `display:none`. So an in-harness scenario that captures a reference before the change and
+compares it after reproduces failures 1 and 2; a visibility scenario reproduces failure 3. A
+behavioural scenario is the right tool; no source-level conformance check is required.
 
 ## Decision
 
-Pin all five as product-behaviour scenarios in `features/todo-dom-stability.feature` and list them in
-`watchbill.json` (watch1: identity for 1-4; watch2: editing visibility for 5). The Quartermaster
-authors the three new step definitions; the Crew fixes `js/app.js` to mutate list elements in place
-and to hide the toggle during edit. No production code written here.
+Pin all three as product-behaviour scenarios in `features/todo-dom-stability.feature`:
+- watch1: element identity for mark and un-mark complete (failures 1 and 2).
+- watch2: label hidden while editing (failure 3).
+
+The Quartermaster authors the one missing step definition ("the label of the todo {string} is not
+visible", mirroring the existing toggle-visibility step) and the Crew fixes `js/app.js` to mutate
+list elements in place on completion changes and to hide the label (and the rest of the view) while
+editing. No production code written here.
 
 ## Open question
 
-Failure 5's scenario fails on current code in-harness (toggle is visible). Whether it also turns
-green after a CSS-class fix depends on whether the harness applies the stylesheet for `checkVisibility`;
-the real-browser acceptance suite is the authority for that behaviour. If the in-harness cannot see a
-CSS-based fix, that is the expected browser-only limitation; do not force it.
+Whether failure 3's in-harness scenario goes green after the fix depends on the Crew hiding the label
+through a channel happy-dom's `getComputedStyle` honours (inline `display:none`), which also satisfies
+the real browser. If a fix leans only on an external stylesheet the in-harness tier cannot load, that
+is the expected browser-only limitation; the real-browser acceptance suite remains the authority.
